The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) calls this one known parser issue with one
fix queued. Its rendered cause table contradicts that conclusion. Split the parser and form
hint problems into separate tickets; fixing the parser alone does not correct the hint.

<Callout kind="warn">
This review describes the 4 October export, not a live queue. Counts below come from report
IDs, tenant settings, and the exported classification code at render time. They are filing
assignments, not confirmation that every report has been reproduced in the product.
</Callout>

## What belongs in each ticket

**Parser:** the shared parser reads dates month first although the tenant writes day first.
App and email reports, and booking-v2 form reports, take this path for the tenants in this
export. For example, Harbour Dental's app report R-41200 says 7 April became 4 July.
With its en-GB tenant settings, `07/04/2026` must stay 7 April, not become 4 July.

**Form hint:** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` while the form reads the input in the tenant's
day-first order. This affects form reports from Alder Physio (de-DE), Copper Studio (es-ES),
and Pale Harbour (pt-PT). R-41241 says an Alder client booked for 6 August arrived on
8 June: following the hint produces `08/06/2026`, which the form reads as 8 June.
The app and email channels for those same tenants still belong to the parser ticket.

Use the tenant's locale and form version from `src/lib/data/tenants`, together with the
report's channel. `meta.browserLocale` describes the reporter's browser, and
`meta.intakeTag` is intake's guess. Neither determines the cause.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

<IncidentEvidence />

## What the timeline shows

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Read the received and merged series separately. Reports were already arriving in April;
the large July merge records accumulated reports entering the same ticket. Neither a bulk
merge nor the status “known” establishes when the defect began, that there is only one
defect, or that a fix has shipped. Reports continue after the merge.

## Replace subject-only triage with evidence

The [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) still tells the desk to merge matching subjects and to
merge when unsure. Its old review date triggers the layout's stale warning. The accepted
[decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) records the original choice and its
tradeoff; keep that historical record unchanged.

For this incident, route by demonstrated cause instead:

1. Deduplicate export rows by report ID before counting reports.
2. Look up the tenant, date order, form version, and channel. Read the body and record the
   input date and observed result when available.
3. Merge only when the evidence establishes the same cause. Subject wording can differ;
   matching subjects can still describe different causes.
4. Keep unknown tenants and unresolved mechanisms separate for investigation. Do not treat
   two missing classifications as the same cause.
5. Keep the one-day-move reports linked to their current filing assignment while checking
   their mechanism. A new finding may require reclassification and revised ticket counts.
6. Before operational use, compare this snapshot with the current desk queue and tenant
   configuration. Refresh the export and rebuild the page when either changes.

The explorer below executes `sameCause` from the export. App and email for Kettle Books
merge despite different subjects. Alder Physio's form and app stay apart despite a matching
subject. Copper Studio's booking-v3 form and Morrow Tax's booking-v2 form also stay apart.

<DedupeExplorer />

Adopt a new decision record if the desk accepts this replacement rule, then update the
triage guidance and its review date. This review does not silently rewrite an accepted
decision or claim the product defects have been fixed.

## Evidence and limits

The tables run the supplied `uniqueById`, `causeOf`, `groupByCause`, and tenant lookup.
The chart groups `receivedAt` and `mergedAt` by the desk's local date, Monday to Sunday.
The supplemental evidence uses `desk-notes.json` without subtracting its reports twice.
No product runtime or fix is included in the export; the ticket reproductions are checks
for engineering to run, supported by report examples and the supplied model.

